## Two skills tend to take over your whole routine

Language fluency isn't one skill — it's four: listening, speaking, reading, and writing. Most study routines end up leaning hard on two of them without anyone deciding that on purpose, and mine did exactly that. Early on, I knew speaking mattered most for actually sounding fluent, so I built everything around it — apps designed for spoken practice, then a weekly hour with a tutor. Reading and writing kept getting quietly pushed to "later."

[[note: A second language isn't just a personal milestone. It can open job opportunities, make studying or working abroad realistic, and expand who you can talk to without a translator in the room.]]

The fix for the gap turned out to be sitting in a tool I already had. I'd been paying for AI subscriptions like Claude and ChatGPT for other things, and it hadn't occurred to me they could double as a reading and writing practice partner — until I opened a fresh chat one day, typed a sentence in Chinese, and asked it to translate what I'd written, flag what I'd gotten wrong, and correct it.

---

## How to build your own AI reading and writing session

**1. Set the rules before you start typing.** Tell the AI you want a translation plus corrections, not a smooth, forgiving back-and-forth — something like "translate what I wrote, tell me what I got wrong, and correct it."

**2. Type in the language, not in English.** Writing the first sentence in Chinese instead of English forces you to think in the language from the start, instead of translating in your head.

**3. Use your actual keyboard for tone marks.** Typing in pinyin with a Chinese keyboard means picking a tone for every word — first, second, third, fourth — which turns into unplanned tone practice on top of the writing.

**4. Ask about structure, not just vocabulary.** The most useful corrections aren't new words — they're catching sentence structure gone wrong without you noticing.

**5. Time-box the session.** Around thirty minutes back and forth is enough to get real correction without it turning into an open-ended chat.

If you want a book alongside this: [*Fluent Forever*](https://link.amazon/B0fdF9jdQ) by Gabriel Wyner is the one worth picking up — it's built around the same idea of using tech and repetition to train pronunciation and reading early, instead of saving them for later.

---

## The reminder

==Fluency isn't one skill — it's four, and most routines only train two of them.== The fix isn't a bigger budget or a new app. It's noticing which two you've been skipping, and building a deliberate space — even a free one — to close that gap.

---

[[signoff: If you're learning a language right now, take stock of which of the four skills you've been quietly skipping — then find a free way to close that gap this week.]]
